.TH CHTTP_REQUEST_SET_HEADER 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttp_request_set_header \- set or replace a request header
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttp_request_set_header(chttp_request_t *req,
                                       const char *name,
                                       const char *value);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Inserts or replaces the named header in the request.  The header name
is normalized to lowercase before storage so that all subsequent
lookups are case-insensitive.  If a header with the same name (ignoring
case) already exists it is overwritten with the new value.

The headers map is lazily created on the first call.

name and value must not contain an embedded CR or LF byte.  The
serializer writes "name: value\r\n" onto the wire with no escaping, so
an unvalidated CR/LF would let a caller that reflects untrusted data
(a forwarded token, a proxied header) into a header inject arbitrary
extra header lines, or split the request into two (HTTP request
splitting).  Such a name or value is rejected outright with
ccol_invalid_args rather than being stored.

A name of "Transfer-Encoding" (case-insensitive) is also rejected
outright.  chttpclient never transfer-codes a request body: a
body-carrying request is always sent whole, Content-Length-framed.
Accepting a caller-set Transfer-Encoding header would let
chttpclient_do's own automatic Content-Length synthesis add a
Content-Length header alongside it on the wire, over a body that was
never actually transfer-coded; an ambiguous framing this library's own
request/response parser rejects outright when it appears on a message
being parsed (RFC 7230 SS3.3.3).

A "Content-Length" header IS accepted by this function (it has no
body length to check the value against without also duplicating the
request's eventual method/redirect context), but is validated later,
when the request actually reaches the wire: chttpclient_do and every
other request-execution function reject a body-carrying request
(POST, PUT, or PATCH) whose caller-set Content-Length does not
exactly match the body actually being sent, for the identical
"declared framing disagrees with the wire" reason Transfer-Encoding
is rejected outright above; see chttpclient_do(3).
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
req     Request to modify; must not be NULL.
name    Header name (e.g. "Authorization"); must not be NULL, must not
        contain a CR or LF byte, and must not be "Transfer-Encoding".
value   Header value; must not be NULL, and must not contain a CR or
        LF byte.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success            Header was set.
ccol_invalid_args       req, name, or value is NULL, name/value
                        contains an embedded CR or LF byte, or name is
                        "Transfer-Encoding".
ccol_not_enough_memory  Map allocation failed.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttp_request_get_header (3)
